:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"ST082\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer treats seed as 'invested capital receiving ETF shares, not launch expenditure' and tracks it separately. It does not say that the legal minimum (the §14(a) $100,000 net-worth condition) is not a commercial launch budget or break-even level.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"It names who pays what. One-time costs are 'normally sponsor.' Recurring operating costs, including trustees, fall to the 'sponsor/adviser under the unitary arrangement.' The fund or shareholders pay the unitary fee plus prospectus-defined exclusions. It also says to 'Confirm actual exclusions and contractual responsibility.'\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"It lists one-time costs (formation, counsel, filings, listing/setup) and recurring costs (platform, servicing, index, exchange, distribution). It states 'Break-even AUM equals annual fixed sponsor costs divided by a positive retained fee margin after variable/platform/advisory costs.'\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Coverage totals 2.5 of 3 and there is no central error. Seed is explicitly not an expenditure. The answer also says launch-cost recovery needs additional cumulative profit, so it does not claim a positive margin guarantees profit. I found no material local falsehood. The grade is partial because point 1's minimum is incomplete: the legal minimum versus commercial launch budget distinction is missing.\"}","ttft_ms":4926,"type":"result","duration_ms":9081,"uuid":"01dd17db-2e2c-4866-ba05-16b9c9dff8b8","ttft_stream_ms":1699,"time_to_request_ms":52,"first_content_frame_ms":1700,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
